@prefix ds: <https://ds.canonical.com/>.
@prefix anatomy: <https://anatomy.canonical.com/>.
@prefix dt: <https://dt.canonical.com/>.

ds:concept.Foundations-Spacing.-Element-vs-container-owned a ds:Concept;
    ds:name "Foundations: Spacing. Element vs container-owned";
    ds:summary "This document covers both inter-element spacing (gaps between components and patterns) and intra-component spacing (internal padding and border compensation):\n\n- Vertical and horizontal spacing architecture\n- When and why to use each spacing model\n- Baseline grid and nudge token architecture\n- Invariants that both models must preserve";
    ds:tier ds:global;
    ds:content "\n\n**Status:** Final  \n**Version:** 1.0  \n**Last updated:** 2026-04-24\n\n\n\n## Table of contents\n\n1. [Glossary](#glossary)\n2. [Problem statement and scope](#1-problem-statement-and-scope)\n3. [The vertical spacing architecture](#2-the-vertical-spacing-architecture)\n4. [Horizontal spacing](#3-horizontal-spacing)\n5. [Invariants](#4-invariants)\n\n\n\n## Glossary\n\nCopy of Copy of Table 10\n\n\n\n## 1. Problem statement and scope\n\n### 1.1 The problem\n\nOur Design System serves three fundamentally different tiers:\n\n- Application UIs – dashboards, forms, settings pages, admin panels (MAAS, Landscape, Launchpad, Snapcraft, support portal, and ~50 other products)\n- Editorial / marketing sites – [ubuntu.com](http://ubuntu.com), [canonical.com](http://canonical.com), canonical.design, blog content\n- Documentation\n\nThese tiers have different spacing needs. Application UIs benefit from explicit, container-controlled spacing. Editorial content and documentation benefit from semantic, element-owned spacing that preserves typographic hierarchy automatically.\n\nNo single spacing model optimally serves all tiers. A system that forces one model everywhere either frustrates the majority use case (application teams) or sacrifices semantic correctness for editorial and documentation tiers.\n\n### 1.2 Scope\n\nThis specification covers both inter-element spacing (gaps between components and patterns) and intra-component spacing (internal padding and border compensation):\n\n- Vertical and horizontal spacing architecture\n- When and why to use each spacing model\n- Baseline grid and nudge token architecture\n- Invariants that both models must preserve\n\nOut of scope:\n\n- Typography specification (referenced where relevant)\n- Grid system specification (referenced where relevant)\n- Implementation details (CSS architecture, component APIs, tooling)\n\n### 1.3 Goals\n\n1. **Predictability:** Spacing behaviour is deterministic and learnable\n2. **Composability:** Elements compose correctly in both models\n3. **Maintainability:** Spacing changes propagate through tokens, not through container dependencies\n4. **Tier fit:** Each tier uses the model best suited to it\n\n\n\n## 2. Vertical spacing\n\n### 2.1 Thesis\n\nVertical spacing should be owned by elements in content flow and owned by containers in application UIs. This split keeps editorial spacing typographically precise and application spacing simple.\n\nMature design systems implicitly follow this split: [GOV.UK](https://design-system.service.gov.uk/styles/spacing/) uses element-owned spacing for typography; [Material Design 3](https://m3.material.io/foundations/layout/understanding-layout/spacing), [Shopify Polaris](https://polaris.shopify.com/design/layout/spacial-organization), and [Atlassian](https://atlassian.design/foundations/spacing) use container-owned spacing for application UIs. Making this distinction explicit gives us semantic correctness for content and simple composability for UI composition.\n\nCopy of Copy of Table 11\n\n### 2.2 The two modes\n\nCopy of Table 27\n\n\n\n### 2.3 Element-owned spacing\n\nBy default, every element carries semantic margin-bottom. This ensures:\n\n- Developers not familiar with the DS get working spacing immediately\n- Prototyping is straightforward – drop elements on a page and they don’t collide\n- No container boilerplate required for simple pages\n\n2.3.1 Core principle\n\nEach element owns the space below it. Elements declare `margin-bottom` only; no element declares `margin-top`. This follows Harry Roberts’ “[single-direction margin declarations](https://csswizardry.com/2012/06/single-direction-margin-declarations/)” pattern.\n\nThe single-direction rule applies to spacing between elements only: elements use `margin-bottom` (never `margin-top`). The last-child reset (see §2.3.5) prevents external margins and container spacing from doubling up. Internal spacing inside components is handled with padding on any side (for example buttons, inputs, strips, and containers).\n\n**Why bottom (not top):**\n\n- **Resilience to unknown elements.** Content is authored top-down. If an unstyled element enters the flow (a D3 canvas, an embedded iframe, a third-party widget), it arrives with `margin-top: 0`. With bottom-only, the preceding element already declared appropriate space below itself – the unknown element gets reasonable separation for free. With top-only, the unknown element would need to declare its own space above, but since the system doesn’t style it, it won’t. The result: the unknown element crowds whatever is above it.  \n  \nComparison:  \n*[Example: button followed by an unstyled D3 canvas – bottom-only provides separation vs top-only where the canvas crowds the button]*  \n\n- **Industry precedent.** InDesign uses “Space After” as the primary typographic control; “Space Before” is avoided because it creates ambiguity at the tops of frames. Figma auto-layout applies gap after each item. [GOV.UK](https://design-system.service.gov.uk/styles/spacing/) typography applies `margin-top: 0` and relies primarily on bottom spacing for flow:\n\n2.3.2 How it works\n\nEvery text element and component ships with a semantic `margin-bottom`:\n\n```\nh1      →  margin-bottom based on its role in hierarchy\nh2      →  margin-bottom based on its role in hierarchy\np       →  margin-bottom based on its role in hierarchy\nbutton  →  margin-bottom based on its role in hierarchy\n```\n\nThe value is determined by the element’s semantic role and visual weight, drawn from the spacing token scale.\n\nElements like buttons may appear at different hierarchy levels (inside cards, at the end of forms, or inside hero sections). In element-owned mode, the button keeps the same semantic `margin-bottom` token across contexts. If a section needs extra separation from the next section, that separation is owned by the parent container (via its own padding or margin), not by changing button spacing. In container-owned mode, the container’s gap controls all spacing and button `margin-bottom` is reset to `0`.\n\n\n\n*[Example: stacked elements with margin-bottom showing semantic spacing values]*\n\n2.3.3 Why this model suits editorial content\n\nIn editorial/content flow, elements are heterogeneous – headings, paragraphs, images, pull quotes, lists. The spacing transition between a heading and a paragraph is semantically different from the transition between two paragraphs.\n\n```\nh2       →  larger gap (topic change)\np        →  standard gap (continuation)\np        →  standard gap (continuation)\nfigure   →  larger gap (visual break)\nh2       →  larger gap (new topic)\n```\n\nA parent container cannot manage these transitions without encoding knowledge of every child type and order. Element-owned spacing handles this automatically because each element carries its own contextually appropriate spacing.\n\nThis principle extends beyond text elements to higher-level layout devices: sections, figures, and content blocks also carry their own `margin-bottom`, establishing spacing at every level of the document hierarchy.\n\n**Real-world examples:**\n\n- Traditional publishing – element-intrinsic vertical spacing is the norm, implemented via space before/after in paragraph styles for example.\n- Browser defaults and markdown renderers – rely on element-owned margins\n\n2.3.4 Where it applies\n\nElement-owned spacing is the system default. It applies everywhere unless a container explicitly takes over. It is the primary model for:\n\n- Marketing pages with mixed content\n- Blog posts and articles\n- Documentation with semantic heading hierarchy\n- Rich text / markdown-rendered content\n\nThe choice between element-owned and container-owned spacing is made at the **product level** – an entire site or application commits to one mode. Mixing modes within a single product would create inconsistent vertical rhythm and complicate nudge token management, since baseline alignment relies on a single consistent spacing strategy per codebase.\n\n2.3.5 Last-child handling\n\nIn element-owned mode, the last child in a content-flow container still carries its `margin-bottom`. To prevent double spacing (element margin + container padding), the design system’s base CSS automatically resets trailing margins at each flow boundary:\n\n```css\n/* Working selector name for illustration; not a prescriptive API */\n.content-flow-container > :last-child {\n  margin-bottom: 0;\n}\n```\n\nNote: This is not a manual step. The rule is part of the system’s CSS foundation. In nested layouts (for example `container > grid-row > col > h2` and `col > p`), each column that hosts content flow should be a content-flow container so each branch resets its own trailing margin.\n\n**Component implementations:** when text elements are wrapped as components (e.g. `<Heading>`, `<Paragraph>`), the last-child reset should be handled by the content-flow container, not by individual components. Components should not need a `:last` variant – the container’s `:last-child` selector targets whatever element renders last, regardless of component wrapper. If a framework prevents CSS child selectors from reaching the rendered element, the container component should apply the reset via its own logic.\n\nThe default reset uses `margin-bottom: 0`. A precision variant that preserves baseline compensation is described in §2.6.2.\n\n**Example: Hero pattern**\n\n```\nHero (padding-bottom: 64px)\n├── h1 \"Sign up today\"       (margin-bottom: 24px)\n├── p  \"Get started free\"    (margin-bottom: 16px)\n├── [Button: Sign up]         (margin-bottom: 0)   ← last-child reset\n└── (hero padding-bottom provides the 64px separation to next section)\n\n```\n\nWithout the reset, the button’s `margin-bottom` (16px) would stack with the hero’s `padding-bottom` (64px), producing 80px – a double-spacing bug. The automatic reset eliminates this.\n\n### 2.4 Container-owned spacing\n\n2.4.1 Core principle\n\nContainers define spacing between their children. Children inside a container have their `margin-bottom` set to zero; the container controls gaps through `gap` values. Elements are spacing-neutral – the container owns all inter-child spacing.\n\n2.4.2 How it works\n\nA container resets child spacing and applies uniform (or structured) gaps:\n\n```\nContainer with gap (16px)\n  ├── TextField    (margin-bottom: 0)\n  ├── TextField    (margin-bottom: 0)\n  └── Button       (margin-bottom: 0)\n```\n\nThe container takes ownership. Elements become spacing-neutral.  \nRecommended safeguard: apply an app-scope element qualifier margin-reset so browser default element margins do not reintroduce element-owned spacing.\n\n2.4.3 Why this model suits application UI\n\nIn application UI, we prioritise simplicity: spacing is owned by containers rather than by individual element semantics.\n\nContainer-owned spacing provides:\n\n- Visible spacing intent – inspect the parent to see spacing decisions\n- Predictable reordering – move children without spacing side effects\n- Density control – choose a container with the desired density level\n- Consolidated control – spacing is configured at the container level\n\n**Nested containers for varying density:**\n\nWhen different parts of an interface need different density, nest containers with different gap values. Each container owns the spacing for its direct children – no element-level margin decisions are needed.\n\n```\nForm (padding: 16px, gap: 16px)\n├── Field group (gap: 4px)            ← dense: label, input, helper text\n│   ├── Label: \"Upstream DNS\"\n│   ├── Input: \"8.8.8.8\"\n│   └── Helper: \"Comma-separated list of upstream DNS servers.\"\n└── Field group (gap: 4px)            ← same dense group, separated by larger gap\n    ├── Label: \"Default TTL\"\n    ├── Input: \"30\"\n    └── Helper: \"...\"\n```\n\nIn this example, the form’s `gap` separates the two field groups with comfortable spacing. Inside each field group, a small gap keeps the label, input, and helper text tightly bound as a single unit. The form container owns the inter-group spacing; each field group owns its internal density.\n\n**Real-world examples:**\n\n- Material Design 3 – spacing defined via grid and structural primitives\n- Shopify Polaris – Stack primitive controls vertical spacing\n- Atlassian Design System – Stack primitive defines spacing between children\n- Chakra UI – Stack component exists specifically to control spacing\n\n### 2.4.4 Application-specific container provisions\n\nOne container pattern is unique to application layouts:\n\n**Fixed-height status bar.** A status bar is simply a container with a fixed height. Its width can span both the main area and side drawers, or be scoped to the main area only – depending on use case. Like all application containers, it resets child margins to zero and owns the spacing between its children.\n\n### 2.5 Intra-component vertical padding\n\nIntra-component vertical padding is component-owned: each component sets its internal top/bottom padding regardless of context.\n\nFor example, buttons, inputs, and cards keep the same internal top/bottom padding whether they appear alone or inside grouped layouts.\n\nWhen borders change the box model, adjust top/bottom padding so outer component height remains aligned to whole-`bU` steps.\n\n### 2.6 Baseline grid and nudges\n\n2.6.1 The baseline grid\n\nIn our design system, the default baseline unit is 4px (previously 8px).  \nWe use the acronym `bU` throughout this specification.\n\n> **Box model consideration:** When elements have borders, the entire box model must be accounted for in height calculations. For example, an element with `padding: 16px` and `border: 1px` should reduce padding to `14px` to maintain the same total height. This principle applies to both spacing modes.\n\n2.6.2 The nudge technique\n\nThe underlying issue is browser line-height centering: CSS centres text vertically within the line-height box rather than anchoring it to a baseline. This means stacking text elements of different sizes produces baselines that land at unpredictable positions – not on any consistent grid.\n\nNudge tokens solve this by adding a small `padding-top` to push each text element’s baseline onto the grid, with a corresponding increase in `margin-bottom` to compensate.\n\nNudges are text-level only. They apply to typographic elements and text-bearing components (for example buttons, inputs, list items, etc), not containers. Containers remain spacing-neutral and use semantic spacing tokens; this preserves substitutability when elements are swapped.\n\nBaseline compensation model:\n\n- `padding-top = nudge`\n- `baseline-compensation = bU - nudge`\n- `margin-bottom = semantic-spacing + baseline-compensation`\n\nFlow-boundary precision reset (for non-zero nudge contexts):\n\n```css\n\n/* Each text element sets semantic spacing and compensation values. Illustration in px for clarity, real values are rem and come from tokens, not css vars */\np {\n  --spAfter-sem: var(--space-after-semantic-p);   /* e.g. 16px */\n  --spAfter-comp: var(--space-after-comp-p);      /* e.g. 6.4px */\n}\n\nh1 {\n  --spAfter-sem: var(--space-after-semantic-h1);  /* e.g. 16px */\n  --spAfter-comp: var(--space-after-comp-h1);     /* e.g. 2px */\n}\n\n.content-flow-container > * {\n  margin-bottom: calc(var(--spAfter-sem, 0px) + var(--spAfter-comp, 0px));\n}\n\n.content-flow-container > :last-child {\n  margin-bottom: var(--spAfter-comp, 0px);\n}\n```\n\nThis approach keeps baseline continuity at flow boundaries.  \nImplementation can be provided through any equivalent mechanism (for example a prose wrapper component, a utility class, or a scoped element qualifier).\n\n**Last-child reset options**\n\n`Option A (default, simpler):`  \nKeep the boundary reset as `margin-bottom: 0` (as described in §2.3.5).\n\n`Option B (precision):`  \nUse the precision reset above, which preserves `spAfter` compensation on the last child and trims only semantic spacing. This keeps baselines aligned even at flow boundaries.\n\n2.6.3 Scope\n\nThe nudge technique is reserved for Editorial / marketing and Documentation tiers, where calculated nudge values are used for content-flow typography. Application UI tier is exempt, and all nudge tokens are intentionally set to `0`.\n\n2.6.4 Trade-off summary\n\nCopy of Copy of Table 13\n\n\n\n## 3. Horizontal spacing\n\nHorizontal spacing uses a container-owned model in both vertical modes.\n\nUnlike vertical spacing, horizontal spacing rarely encodes semantic hierarchy or visual weight transitions between adjacent elements. Large separations are typically set by the grid (gutters, margins, keylines). What remains is spacing between smaller grouped elements inside components and patterns, where container-defined gaps are simpler and more consistent.\n\n### 3.1 Layout-level spacing\n\nHorizontal spacing at the layout level is handled by the grid system:\n\nCopy of Copy of Table 14\n\nThe grid provides consistent horizontal rhythm across breakpoints and establishes keylines: shared horizontal offsets that align text and controls across components and patterns, improving scannability. Components placed within grid columns inherit this spacing automatically.\n\n> **Two-dimensional grids:** When layout produces a two-dimensional grid of items (card grids, dashboards, image galleries), the vertical gap between rows must equal the horizontal gutter (24px). This ensures items sit on a uniform spatial grid in both axes.\n\n### 3.2 Horizontal sibling spacing\n\nHorizontal spacing between sibling elements is container-owned: the parent context sets the gap.\n\nFor example, in a row of buttons or chips, the container defines the horizontal gap between items:\n\n\n\n### 3.3 Intra-component horizontal padding\n\nIntra-component horizontal padding is component-owned: each component sets its internal left/right padding regardless of context.\n\nFor example, buttons, inputs, and chips keep the same internal left/right padding whether they appear alone or inside grouped layouts.:  \n\n\nWhen borders change the box model, adjust left/right padding to preserve content box width. For bottom-only borders, left/right padding stays unchanged.\n\nCopy of Copy of Table 15\n\n\n\n## 4. Invariants\n\nInvariants are the non-negotiable rules that both spacing modes must preserve. They serve as evaluation criteria for architectural decisions and acceptance criteria for contributions.\n\n### 4.1 The invariant list\n\nCopy of Copy of Table 16\n\n### 4.2 How invariants apply to each mode\n\n**Element-owned (default mode):**\n\n- Substitutability is intrinsic – each element brings its own spacing\n- Locality is intrinsic – no parent knowledge required\n- No double spacing is handled by margin-bottom-only rule (no collapsing margin-top)\n\n**Container-owned mode:**\n\n- Substitutability is preserved because all children have margin-bottom: 0; spacing is uniform from the container\n- Locality is preserved – elements don’t need to know their context; the container resets them unconditionally\n- No double spacing is handled by the reset mechanism (children have no margins to double)\n\n### 4.3 Invariant priority\n\nWhen invariants conflict, resolve in this order:\n\n1. **Substitutability** – composition must remain predictable\n2. **Locality** – knowledge shouldn’t leak across boundaries\n3. **No double spacing** – additive bugs break layouts\n4. **Composition isolation** – parent-child contracts must be stable\n5. **Grid alignment** – vertical rhythm should be maintained\n6. **Predictable removal** – overrides must be safe\n\n> **Note on grid alignment:** In zero-nudge mode, grid alignment refers to element heights being whole multiples of `bU`. True baseline alignment (text sitting on grid lines) is only achieved with non-zero nudge values.\n\n\n\n\n\n## 5. Authoring guidance\n\nThis section translates the architectural model into concrete decisions for the two audiences who apply it daily.\n\n### 5.1 Component designers\n\nSpacing behaviour differs between tiers, but the difference is encoded in the token values – not in how components are designed. A component built correctly for one tier will work in the other once the appropriate tokens are applied.\n\nIn editorial and documentation tiers, text inside components aligns to a baseline grid – the same behaviour as the legacy Vanilla framework this design system replaces. Nudge tokens (`padding-top`) shift each text baseline onto the grid, with a corresponding `margin-bottom` compensation. In application UI tiers, nudge tokens are set to zero; internal padding on elements such as buttons is set so that the entire element aligns to the baseline grid, rather than individual text baselines within it.\n\n**Application UI**\n\nContainers own all inter-child spacing. Components should be spacing-neutral.\n\n- `spAfter` (margin-bottom) is set to `0` at the tier level via zero-nudge tokens – no manual work per component.\n- Internal vertical padding is component-owned. Use values from the spacing scale.\n- If the component has a border that changes the box model, reduce padding to keep total height on whole-`bU` steps.\n\n**Editorial and documentation**\n\nComponents carry their own spacing in content flow.\n\n- Set `margin-bottom` using a semantic spacing token. Never use `margin-top`.\n- If the component contains text, apply the nudge token (`padding-top`) for baseline alignment (§2.6.2).\n- Pick the token that matches the element’s visual weight and hierarchy level.\n\n**Both tiers**\n\n- Internal horizontal padding is always component-owned.\n- For all-side borders, reduce horizontal padding by the border width.\n- Keep all internal padding consistent regardless of layout context.\n\n**Pre-review checklist**\n\n- Spacing values use tokens, not arbitrary px\n- No `margin-top` declared anywhere in the component\n- Text elements: nudge token applied if serving editorial/docs tier\n- Component height is whole-`bU` multiples (account for borders)\n- Tested in composition: followed by a heading, a paragraph, and a button\n\n### 5.2 Pattern designers\n\nPatterns compose components. The principle: patterns don’t re-encode spacing that belongs to components or containers.\n\n**Application UI patterns**\n\nUse container-owned spacing (§2.4).\n\n- Set a gap value on the container. Component tokens are already zeroed; children will be spacing-neutral.\n- Don’t add `margin-bottom` to individual children.\n- Use nested containers for sections of the pattern that need different density (§2.4.3 for an example).\n- For two-dimensional grids (card grids, tile layouts), set the vertical row gap equal to the horizontal gutter (24px, per §3.1).\n\n**Editorial and documentation patterns**\n\nUse element-owned spacing (§2.3).\n\n- Don’t override child `margin-bottom`. If the gap looks wrong, the component needs adjusting – not the pattern.\n- Wrap the pattern in a content-flow container so the last-child reset applies automatically (§2.3.5).\n\n**Tight compositions**\n\nHero heading pairs, button bars, and key–value rows sometimes need less space than the component default. This is not a pattern-level override.\n\n- Request a spacing variant in the component: minimal mode removes semantic spacing while preserving baseline compensation.\n- The pattern references the component variant; it never applies inline spacing values.\n- Substitutability (§4.1) is preserved: swapping the component still composes correctly.\n\n**Slot-based patterns**\n\nWhen a pattern passes arbitrary children through a slot, it can’t guarantee specific spacing.\n\n- Document the expected child types.\n- Wrap the slot in a content-flow container (editorial/docs) or a gap container (application UI).\n\n\n\n\n\n## Appendices\n\n### A. References\n\n- Harry Roberts: [Single-direction margin declarations](https://csswizardry.com/2012/06/single-direction-margin-declarations/)\n- [GOV.UK Design System: Spacing](https://design-system.service.gov.uk/styles/spacing/)\n- Material Design 3: [Layout spacing](https://m3.material.io/foundations/layout/understanding-layout/spacing#9ebf5386-23ca-400a-b68d-e8c679a4a19d)\n- Shopify Polaris: [Stack](https://shopify.dev/docs/api/checkout-ui-extensions/latest/polaris-web-components/layout-and-structure/stack)\n  - Atlassian Design System: [Stack](https://atlassian.design/components/primitives/stack/)\n\n";
    ds:knownEdgeCases "";
    ds:conceptType ds:concepttype.Explanation.
